      en: "The central Sierra Maestra is the highest and most imposing mountain range in Cuba, stretching along the southeastern coast of the island. This geologically complex region is home to Pico Turquino, which marks the island's highest point at 1,974 meters above sea level. Formed through tectonic uplift, the range is covered today by dense cloud forests and tropical rainforests that harbor exceptional biodiversity. Historically, the Sierra Maestra achieved global fame as the primary refuge and operational base for Fidel Castro's rebel army during the Cuban Revolution in the 1950s. The steep slopes and deep ravines provided strategic advantages for guerrilla warfare, and today they are protected within national parks that attract hikers and history enthusiasts alike. Beyond its historical significance, the range plays a crucial role in the water cycle of the surrounding Santiago de Cuba and Granma provinces. Preserving this ecosystem is vital for the protection of numerous endemic species, including some of the world's smallest amphibians.",
